## Retype

Reclassify a work item to a different type — the sequence number (and durable identity) is
preserved; only the ID prefix changes. All incoming refs, children's parent links, and prose
mentions are rewritten to the new ID atomically.

{% set retype_targets = [] %}
{% for item_type, item_spec in spec.items.items() %}
{% if item_spec.category != "roster" %}
{% set _ = retype_targets.append(item_type) %}
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
```bash
sq <type> <n> retype <new-type>{% if retype_targets | length > 1 %}   # e.g. sq {{ retype_targets[0] }} 7 retype {{ retype_targets[1] }}{% endif +%}
```

Valid targets: {% for item_type in retype_targets %}`{{ item_type }}`{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}.

{# Same-workflow example: group non-roster types by their declared lifecycle name, rather than
   naming specific bundled types — a custom spec may share/split workflows differently. #}
{% set lifecycle_groups = [] %}
{% for item_type, item_spec in spec.items.items() %}
{% if item_spec.category != "roster" %}
{% set ns = namespace(matched=none) %}
{% for g in lifecycle_groups %}
{% if g.lifecycle == item_spec.lifecycle %}
{% set ns.matched = g %}
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% if ns.matched %}
{% set _ = ns.matched.types.append(item_type) %}
{% else %}
{% set _ = lifecycle_groups.append({"lifecycle": item_spec.lifecycle, "types": [item_type]}) %}
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% set shared_groups = [] %}
{% for g in lifecycle_groups %}
{% if g.types | length > 1 %}
{% set _ = shared_groups.append(g.types | join("↔")) %}
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
**Status behaviour:** when the old and new types share the same workflow{% if shared_groups %} (e.g. {{ shared_groups | join(", ") }}){% endif %} the status is carried as-is; otherwise
the status resets to the new type's initial value and the command says so.

**Refusals with actionable hints:**
- item has sub-entities (clear them first)
- existing parent would be invalid for the new type (re-parent or remove the parent first)
- any child would become invalid under the new type (re-parent or remove those children first)

After retype, `sq check` is clean and `sq repair` is a stable no-op.

## Remove vs. Cancel

{# The status names below are spec-derived, not literals: `dropped` is the off-spine settled
   exit of a representative type's own lifecycle (bundled: `Cancelled`), so a squad whose
   lifecycle names that state otherwise reads a command that runs. The surrounding narrative
   is deliberately static — only the vocabulary moves. #}
{% set _drop_anchor = cheatsheet_anchor_type(spec) %}
{% set dropped = spec.first_dropped_status(_drop_anchor) if _drop_anchor else none %}
Two distinct exit paths for work items — use the right one:

| | Cancel | Remove |
|---|---|---|
| **Intent** | Work genuinely considered, then dropped | Item should never have existed (mis-creation, test artifact, rolled-back decision) |
| **Effect** | Status → `{{ dropped or "<dropped-status>" }}`; item stays on the books, greppable, linkable, visible in `tree`/`list` | File deleted, index entry gone; only a sequence-number gap remains |
| **Command** | `sq <type> <n> status {{ dropped or "<dropped-status>" }}` | `sq <type> <n> remove` |

```bash
sq <type> <n> status {{ dropped or "<dropped-status>" }}   # drop work that was genuinely considered
sq <type> <n> remove             # erase a mis-creation (interactive confirm)
sq <type> <n> remove --yes       # skip the confirm
sq <type> <n> remove --force     # also sever incoming refs from referrers' frontmatter
```

**Ref and child safety:**
- `remove` refuses when the item has incoming refs or children, listing every offender.
- `--force` severs refs but still refuses while children exist; re-parent or remove children first.
- After any removal `sq check` is clean — no dangling refs, no dangling parent links.

**Sequence gaps are sanctioned, not corruption.** Removal deletes the index entry but never
touches the counter high-water mark — the freed number is never reissued.  A gap means "an item
with that sequence number existed and was removed."  `sq check` and `sq repair` treat gaps as
normal; the reflog records a reconstructable removal line that explains each gap.

## Duplicate numbers across trees

The counter that mints sequence numbers lives in each tree's own index, so a worktree, a branch
or a second clone allocates from its own sequence knowing nothing of the others.  Two trees that
both create work therefore hand out the same number, and merging them lands both files in one
folder — one number, two items.  Recognise it by the number rather than by an error: the same
sequence number turns up on two items (often under two different prefixes), or an ID resolves to
something you did not expect.  Two verbs, one for each side of the merge:

```bash
sq renumber --from <n> --onto <other-counter>   # before the merge: shift this tree's block clear
sq repair --renumber                            # after the merge: reassign whatever arrived twice
```

`sq renumber` is the planned move, run deliberately in one tree while the trees are still
separate: every local item numbered at or above `--from` shifts into a range above both counters,
so nothing can collide.  `sq repair --renumber` is the recovery once the collision is already on
disk: for each number held more than once it keeps one item and mints a fresh number for the
rest, rewriting the refs, parent links, prose mentions and filenames that pointed at them, then
rebuilds the index.

## Ref kinds

{# The kinds table is generated from the merged spec's `[ref_kinds]`, never a hand-written list,
   so a project that declares its own kind sees it here and in the skill text its agents read.
   Meaning is the entry's declared `hint` (falling back to its `label`, which every entry has);
   Consumer is derived from the declared semantic `role` — plus the per-type `ref_rules` that
   name the kind — so no row restates a binding the spec already carries. Every name below the
   table is resolved from the spec for the same reason: a project may rename any of them. #}
{% set _default_kind = spec.ref_kinds_with_role("default") | list | first | default(none) %}
{% set _blocker_kind = spec.dependency_ref_kind("blocker") %}
{% set _dependent_kind = spec.dependency_ref_kind("dependent") %}
{% set _supersession_kind = spec.ref_kinds_with_role("supersession") | list | first | default(none) %}
{% set _superseded_status = spec.statuses_with_role("superseded") | first | default(none) %}
{% set _rule_kinds = [] %}
{% for _item_type in spec.items %}
{% for _rr in spec.item_ref_rules(_item_type) %}
{% if _rr.kind not in _rule_kinds %}
{% set _ = _rule_kinds.append(_rr.kind) %}
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% endfor %}
Ref kinds are declared vocabulary. The bundled set is the default; a project may declare its own, and may rename or drop a built-in it does not use — refused while live refs still carry it. A kind the merged spec does not declare is rejected. Engine behaviour binds to a kind's declared semantic role, never to its name: a renamed dependency kind keeps driving `sq blocked`, and a kind with no semantic is navigational. Use `sq <type> <n> ref add <id> --kind <kind>`; the table below is what this squad declares.

| Kind | Meaning | Consumer |
|---|---|---|
{% for _code, _rk in spec.ref_kinds.items() %}
| `{{ _code }}` | {{ _rk.hint or _rk.label }} | {% if _rk.role == "dependency" %}`sq blocked`{% elif _rk.role == "preload" %}Preload resolver, retirement gate{% elif _rk.role == "supersession" %}`sq check` supersession rule{% elif _code in _rule_kinds %}`sq check` ref-rule warnings{% else %}Navigation{% endif %} |
{% endfor %}

Every edge is stored on the item you add it to — `A <kind> B` lives on A.{% if _blocker_kind and _dependent_kind %} `{{ _blocker_kind }}` and `{{ _dependent_kind }}` are two spellings of the same dependency: use whichever fits your authoring context, and both feed `sq blocked`.{% endif %}{% if _supersession_kind and _superseded_status %} `sq check` reads `{{ _supersession_kind }}` on the newer record and expects the older one at {{ _superseded_status }}.{% endif %}{% if _default_kind %} Bare `ref add <id>` (no `--kind`) resolves to whichever kind declares the default semantic — `{{ _default_kind }}` here — and that edge is stored without its kind, so renaming the default relabels those edges instead of re-pointing them.{% endif %}
